    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0316" n="190" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg190"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">463.00 R 29/163: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-22T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 22,
                                1925—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>52. Please show your 75, January 21, 3 P.M. to Logan<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d133-f1"/> and request him to cable his comment to the Department
                    with particular reference to any right or interest which he may feel the United
                    States has under present circumstances in the bonds in question.</p>
                <p>See L–89 from Logan, February 9, [1924,] 3 P.M. and Department’s L–54, February
                    29, [1924,] 6 P.M.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d133-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d133-f1" n="1" xml:id="d133-f1-content">
                <p>Col. James A. Logan, Jr., American unofficial representative on the Reparation
